{
  "chapter": "retrieval/vector-search",
  "tier": "build",
  "notes": [
    "One function does retrieval: search(query, filters, k, index). Filters scattered across",
    "call sites is how tenancy bugs ship.",
    "Similarity gets you the ordering. Correctness comes from the filter — the current version,",
    "this tenant, this tier, this region.",
    "The version filter is not optional and not a ranking signal. It is applied whether or not",
    "the caller asked for it, exactly like tenancy. Never ask an embedding to prefer the",
    "current version; ask the database.",
    "Three strategies. Post-filtering searches the whole index and then discards, so a",
    "selective filter leaves fewer than k. Pre-filtering is exact and scans every matching row.",
    "In-algorithm filtering walks only the matching subgraph, so a selective filter makes it",
    "faster rather than worse.",
    "The axis for choosing an engine is filter selectivity, not corpus size.",
    "Embeddings are one-dimensional integers here and distance is the absolute difference, so",
    "the ordering is exact and identical in both languages. Ties break on id."
  ],
